#: R/logcheck.b.R
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%%; box-sizing: border-box;\"><p "
"style=\"font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 0.10em 0; line-height: "
"1.25;\">AssumptionsLab</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.35em 0; line-height: "
"1.25;\">Assumption check for logistic regression</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 "
"0.25em 0;\"> </p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.55em 0; line-height: 1.35;\">Use "
"this analysis when you want to review whether a logistic regression model "
"has defensible methodological assumptions. The goal is not only to compute "
"tests, but to help justify the statistical decision with evidence obtained "
"from your own data.</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.25em 0;\"> </p>%s</div>"
msgstr ""

#: assumptionLibrary/results/intro.content
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box; "
"text-align: justify;\"><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">This library "
"summarizes the assumptions and tests used across AssumptionsLab. Its "
"purpose is not to analyze data directly, but to serve as a methodological "
"guide for interpreting the tests within each analysis.</p><p style=\"margin: "
"0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Core principle:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 "
"0 0.6em 0;\">The library explains what each assumption means and when it is "
"used. Each analysis then interprets its own results using the user's actual "
"data.</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Bibliography: the complete "
"reference list in APA 7th edition, with each source's bibliometric profile, "
"is available in <a "
"href=\"https://github.com/ArchieJamDev/AssumptionsLab/blob/main/docs/Bibliography.md\">docs/Bibliography.md</a>.</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">General rule for "
"p-values:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">p &lt; .05 indicates a "
"statistically significant deviation from the assumption being evaluated. p "
"&gt;= .05 indicates that the result is compatible with the assumption being "
"approximately met.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">Important:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Compatible does not "
"mean proven. It means that, with this data and this test, no statistically "
"significant deviation is observed.</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Avoid "
"interpreting assumption tests mechanically. The decision should combine "
"p-values, sample size, plots, design, and substantive judgment.</p></div>"
msgstr ""

#: assumptionLibrary/results/notes.content
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box; "
"text-align: justify;\"><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Use this library as a "
"general methodological reference. Use the specific analyses to interpret "
"results with your own data.</p></div>"
msgstr ""

#: assumptionLibrary/results/linearityGuide2.content
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box; "
"text-align: justify;\"><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">Bivariate correlation:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Describes "
"simple linear association between two variables. It is exploratory and does "
"not replace diagnosing the full model.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em "
"0; font-weight: 700;\">Distance correlation (dCor) and copula entropy:</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Pearson's r only detects linear association. "
"dCor (Székely et al., 2007) detects linear and non-linear association "
"equally, without assuming a functional form. Copula entropy (Ma &amp; Sun, "
"2011) is an additional dependence measure, free of distributional "
"assumptions, based on the copula of the variables. A pair with dCor notably "
"higher than its Pearson |r| (gap &gt; .10) is a signal, not proof, of a "
"non-linear relationship that a linear model might be missing; it is worth "
"confirming with a scatterplot before concluding non-linearity. The p-value "
"for dCor and for copula entropy is computed by permutation, so its "
"resolution depends on the number of permutations used.</p><p style=\"margin: "
"0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Exploratory quadratic term:</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Adds x² to the model as an additional "
"predictor and compares it against the original model. p &lt; .05 on the x² "
"coefficient suggests possible curvature not captured by the linear "
"relationship. It is a quick, one-predictor-at-a-time check; it does not "
"replace RESET, which evaluates the full model.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 "
"0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Box-Tidwell — what it assesses:</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Evaluates whether a specific numeric predictor "
"would need a power transformation (a form of type Xᵏ) to relate linearly to "
"the dependent variable or, in logistic regression, to the logit. The "
"mechanism: an x × ln(x) interaction term is added to the model alongside x "
"itself, and the significance of that term's coefficient is tested. H0: x "
"does not need to be transformed (the relationship is already approximately "
"linear on the current scale). H1: the relationship would improve with a "
"power transformation of x. Requires x &gt; 0, because the term uses ln(x); "
"it cannot be applied to predictors with negative or zero values without "
"shifting them first.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">Box-Tidwell — when to use it:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">It "
"is the reference test for evaluating logit-linearity of a continuous "
"numeric predictor in logistic regression — unlike the quadratic term or a "
"scatterplot, it directly evaluates the correct scale of the predictor (log, "
"root, linear) instead of only detecting curvature. It is also useful in "
"linear regression when a specific predictor, not the full model, is "
"suspected to be the source of the non-linearity.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em "
"0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Box-Tidwell — limitations:</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">It evaluates one predictor at a time, so it "
"says nothing about joint curvature across several predictors or about "
"general model misspecification (that is what Ramsey RESET is for). With 0 "
"of several predictors significant, the correct conclusion is \"no "
"statistical evidence of a deviation from linearity was detected\", not "
"\"linearity is confirmed\" — the test may simply lack power in the available "
"sample.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Ramsey "
"RESET — what it assesses:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Evaluates "
"whether the model, as a whole, is functionally misspecified — that is, "
"whether it fails to capture some systematic form (curvature, interactions, "
"omitted terms) that a model strictly linear in the current predictors "
"cannot represent. The mechanism: powers of the fitted values (typically the "
"square and the cube) are added to the model, and an F-test contrasts "
"whether those powers significantly improve the fit relative to the original "
"model. H0: the original model is correctly specified (the powers add no "
"additional explanation). H1: the model omits some relevant functional "
"form.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Ramsey "
"RESET — when to use it:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">As a global "
"check after reviewing the individual predictors: if Box-Tidwell or the "
"quadratic term do not flag any particular predictor but the overall fit "
"looks poor, RESET can detect non-linearity that only appears in combination "
"across predictors, or in a form (for example, an interaction) that no "
"single per-predictor check captures on its own. It is the most general of "
"the three tests.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">Ramsey RESET — limitations:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">A "
"significant RESET indicates something about the functional form is wrong, "
"but not what: it does not distinguish between genuine curvature, an omitted "
"interaction, an omitted variable correlated with the predictors, or an "
"influential outlier. It is worth reviewing the residual plots and the other "
"linearity tests before deciding which correction to apply.</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Rainbow test — what it "
"assesses:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Evaluates whether the linear "
"fit is stable across the range of fitted values, rather than concentrating "
"the evidence on one specific form of non-linearity. The mechanism: cases "
"are ordered by their fitted value, the central band is taken (by default, "
"around 50% of the cases with intermediate fitted values), and the model is "
"refit using only that central subset. An F-test compares the residual sum "
"of squares of the full model against that of the model fit only to the "
"central band. H0: the linear fit is equally adequate in the central band as "
"in the full set. H1: the fit deteriorates when the extremes are included, "
"suggesting the relationship is not uniformly linear across the full range "
"of the predictors.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">Rainbow test — when to use it:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">It "
"is useful as a complement to RESET when the non-linearity is suspected not "
"to be smooth, global curvature, but rather a relationship that behaves "
"differently at the extremes of the predictor range versus the center — for "
"example, effects that flatten out or accelerate only at extreme values. It "
"requires a reasonable sample (in this module, at least 20 cases) so the "
"central sub-model has enough degrees of freedom.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em "
"0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Common to RESET and Rainbow — a frequent "
"error:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Interpreting a non-significant "
"RESET or Rainbow result as proof of perfect linearity. Both tests have "
"limited power in small samples (they may miss real curvature) and can flag "
"trivial, practically irrelevant curvature as significant in very large "
"samples. Always read them together with the residuals-vs-fitted plot, not "
"in isolation.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">Sample-size criteria:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">With small "
"samples it can be difficult to detect curvature. With large samples, small "
"curvatures can become significant.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; "
"font-weight: 700;\">Interpretation:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">p "
"&lt; .05 on curvature or specification tests suggests a possible deviation "
"from linearity. p &gt;= .05 indicates that the result is compatible with an "
"approximately linear relationship.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; "
"font-weight: 700;\">Methodological decision:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em "
"0;\">Consider polynomial terms, splines, transformations, or non-linear "
"models if there is evidence of substantive curvature.</p></div>"
msgstr ""

#: assumptionLibrary/results/proportionalOddsGuide2.content
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box; "
"text-align: justify;\"><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">Brant — what it evaluates and its limitations:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 "
"0 0.6em 0;\">Tests H0: each predictor's coefficient is equal across every "
"cutpoint (proportional odds), against H1: at least one cutpoint differs "
"(Brant, 1990). It compares the coefficients from separate binary logits fit "
"at each cutpoint against the single coefficient the proportional-odds model "
"reports. It has limited power in small samples or with sparse "
"categories.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">Partial proportional-odds model — what it does and when to use "
"it:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Lets a specific predictor's "
"coefficient vary across cutpoints while every other predictor keeps a "
"single constant coefficient. It is the most surgical option when Brant "
"flags one or two specific predictors rather than a widespread "
"violation.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">Multinomial model — what it does and when to use it:</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Treats every category of the outcome as "
"nominal, with no assumed order or proportionality relationship between "
"coefficients. It is the safer alternative when Brant's omnibus test is "
"significant and affects several predictors at once, at the cost of not "
"exploiting the categories' order.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; "
"font-weight: 700;\">Reporting cutpoint-specific effects — what it does and "
"when to use it:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Instead of forcing a "
"single model, independent binary logits are fit at each cutpoint and a "
"different odds ratio is reported for each. It is a transparent alternative "
"when the goal is describing the pattern of the relationship rather than "
"producing a single parsimonious model.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em "
"0; font-weight: 700;\">Interpretation:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">p "
"&lt; .05 on Brant's omnibus test suggests a statistically significant "
"deviation from proportional odds. p &gt;= .05 indicates the result is "
"compatible with approximate proportional odds.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 "
"0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Methodological decision:</p><p style=\"margin: "
"0 0 0.6em 0;\">If there is evidence against proportional odds, first check "
"which individual predictor(s) Brant's test flags (not just the omnibus). "
"With one or two specific predictors, consider a partial-proportional-odds "
"model; with a widespread violation, consider a multinomial model "
"(multCheck) or report the cutpoint-specific effects separately.</p></div>"
msgstr ""

#: assumptionLibrary/results/homoscedasticityGuide3.content
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box; "
"text-align: justify;\"><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">Breusch-Pagan — what it assesses and when to use it:</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Regresses the squared residuals on the "
"original model's predictors and tests whether that auxiliary regression "
"explains a significantly larger share of variance than zero (Breusch &amp; "
"Pagan, 1979). H0: residual variance is constant (homoscedasticity). H1: "
"residual variance depends on the predictors. It is the default check for "
"linear regression when an approximately linear relationship between "
"variance and predictors is suspected.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em "
"0; font-weight: 700;\">White — what it assesses and when to use it:</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Generalizes Breusch-Pagan by adding squares "
"and cross-products of the predictors to the auxiliary regression, without "
"assuming a specific functional form for the heteroscedasticity (White, "
"1980). It is more general and more suitable when residual variance is "
"suspected to depend on the predictors non-linearly or through interactions, "
"but it loses some power relative to Breusch-Pagan when the real "
"heteroscedasticity is simply linear.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; "
"font-weight: 700;\">Goldfeld-Quandt — what it assesses and when to use "
"it:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Orders observations by a variable "
"(typically a predictor or the fitted values), omits a central band, and "
"compares the residual sum of squares between the low-value and high-value "
"subgroups using an F test (Goldfeld &amp; Quandt, 1965). It is especially "
"useful when variance is suspected to change in a stepped or marked way "
"around an identifiable point of the ordering variable, rather than smoothly "
"and continuously.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">Spearman |residuals| vs fitted — what it assesses and when to use "
"it:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Computes the Spearman rank "
"correlation between the absolute value of the residuals and the fitted "
"values. It is exploratory and nonparametric: it does not assume a specific "
"functional form, only detecting whether error magnitude grows or shrinks "
"monotonically with the predicted value. It is a good visual/numeric "
"complement to Breusch-Pagan and White, not a formal substitute.</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Interpretation "
"(regression tests):</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">p &lt; .05 suggests "
"that residual variance is not constant (heteroscedasticity). p &gt;= .05 "
"indicates that the result is compatible with approximately constant "
"residual variance.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">Methodological decision:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">In group "
"comparisons, if there is evidence of heterogeneity of variance, consider "
"the Welch correction (Welch, 1947) for the t or F statistic, the "
"Brown-Forsythe test itself as an alternative to classic ANOVA, or robust "
"methods. In regression, consider heteroscedasticity-robust HC3 standard "
"errors (MacKinnon &amp; White, 1985), a transformation of the dependent "
"variable, or a model that explicitly models the variance.</p></div>"
msgstr ""

#: assumptionLibrary/results/independenceGuide2.content
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box; "
"text-align: justify;\"><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">Durbin-Watson — what it assesses:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em "
"0;\">Evaluates whether the residuals of a regression show first-order "
"autocorrelation. H0: no first-order autocorrelation. H1: there is "
"first-order autocorrelation, positive or negative. Its statistic ranges "
"roughly from 0 to 4: near 2 indicates no first-order autocorrelation; below "
"2 suggests positive autocorrelation; above 2 suggests negative "
"autocorrelation. There is an approximate relationship DW ≈ 2 × (1 − r1), "
"where r1 is the estimated residual autocorrelation at lag 1.</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Durbin-Watson — when to "
"use it:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">As a simple diagnostic when "
"there is a linear least-squares regression, temporally ordered data, "
"specific interest in the dependence between a residual and the immediately "
"preceding one, and a model without the lagged dependent variable as a "
"regressor.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">Durbin-Watson — when not to choose it:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em "
"0;\">When the model includes the lagged dependent variable (a dynamic "
"model), when autocorrelation of order 2, 3, 12, etc. is suspected, when "
"there is a more complex temporal structure such as seasonality, or when a "
"formal test with an easily generalizable p-value is needed. In modern "
"applied practice, Durbin-Watson is usually reported as a quick descriptive "
"indicator, while Breusch-Godfrey is generally preferred for the formal "
"test.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">Breusch-Godfrey — what it assesses:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em "
"0;\">Also called the LM test for serial correlation, it tests whether the "
"residuals of a regression show autocorrelation up to an order p chosen by "
"the analyst. H0: no residual autocorrelation up to order p. H1: "
"autocorrelation exists in at least one of the lags from 1 to p. The test is "
"based on an auxiliary regression that incorporates the lagged residuals; "
"under H0, the LM statistic approximately follows a chi-square distribution "
"with p degrees of freedom.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; "
"font-weight: 700;\">Breusch-Godfrey — when to use it:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 "
"0 0.6em 0;\">It is the most general option for regressions. It is worth "
"using with econometric regressions, when more than one lag needs to be "
"tested, when the model includes lagged explanatory variables or the lagged "
"dependent variable itself, and when a formal test with a p-value is needed "
"to decide between modeling AR errors, using GLS, applying HAC/Newey-West "
"errors, or rethinking the model's dynamic specification.</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Breusch-Godfrey — how "
"to choose the order p:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">It should not be "
"chosen mechanically or overstated. With annual data it usually makes sense "
"to start with 1 or 2 lags; with quarterly data, try 1 to 4; with monthly "
"data, testing up to 12 can be reasonable if there is a possible annual "
"pattern; with higher frequencies it is worth relying on process knowledge "
"and reviewing the residuals' autocorrelation function (ACF) and partial "
"autocorrelation function (PACF). Practical rule: when in doubt between "
"Durbin-Watson and Breusch-Godfrey for a regression, Breusch-Godfrey is "
"preferred.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">Ljung-Box — what it assesses:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">This "
"is a joint (portmanteau) test that assesses whether the autocorrelations up "
"to a horizon h are jointly zero. H0: no autocorrelation up to lag h. H1: "
"autocorrelation exists in at least one of those lags. Its Q statistic "
"combines, weighted by sample size and each lag, the squares of the "
"estimated residual autocorrelations up to lag h; so it does not ask whether "
"there is autocorrelation only at lag 1, but whether the residuals, taken "
"together, look like white noise up to that horizon.</p><p style=\"margin: "
"0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Ljung-Box — when to use it and which "
"horizon h to choose:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">It is especially "
"useful for diagnosing the residuals of an ARIMA, SARIMA, ETS, or other "
"time-series forecasting model, and for checking whether temporal structure "
"remains unexplained after fitting the model. It is typically applied to the "
"residuals of an already-fitted model, not to the raw series. The horizon h "
"depends on frequency and objective: with quarterly data h=4 or 8 is usually "
"reviewed; with monthly data, h=12 is a natural starting point; with daily "
"data, a horizon consistent with weekly, monthly, or business cycles is "
"worth using. When applying it to the residuals of an ARIMA, the degrees of "
"freedom must also be adjusted for the AR and MA parameters already "
"estimated.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">Ljung-Box — difference from Breusch-Godfrey:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 "
"0.6em 0;\">Both can review several lags at once, but their natural context "
"differs: Breusch-Godfrey is used in a regression with covariates and asks "
"whether that regression's errors are serially correlated; Ljung-Box is a "
"global diagnostic of the residuals of a time-series model and asks whether "
"those residuals look like white noise.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em "
"0; font-weight: 700;\">Runs test — what it actually assesses:</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Unlike the three previous tests, it is not "
"primarily a test of linear autocorrelation. It evaluates whether the order "
"of a sequence looks random. A run is a consecutive sequence of observations "
"of the same class — for example, consecutive positive residuals versus "
"negative ones, or values above versus below the median. H0: the sequence "
"was generated randomly. H1: the sequence does not look random. The test "
"compares the observed number of runs with the number expected under "
"randomness: too few runs suggest clustering or persistence; too many can "
"suggest excessive alternation.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; "
"font-weight: 700;\">Runs test — when to use it:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 "
"0.6em 0;\">When a nonparametric diagnostic is needed, when the residuals are "
"not normal or contain outliers, when the interest is in detecting sign "
"clustering, regime changes, or a general lack of randomness, when working "
"with a binary sequence (signs, pass/fail, defective/non-defective), or as a "
"nonparametric complement to the autocorrelation tests above.</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Runs test — what it "
"does not conclude:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">A significant result "
"only supports that the order of the series does not look random; it does "
"not necessarily imply AR(1)-type linear autocorrelation. It can detect "
"non-randomness arising from trends, structural changes, persistence of "
"signs, or non-linear patterns. Conversely, a series may not show strong "
"linear autocorrelation and still not be completely random.</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Practical decision "
"tree:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Linear regression with temporal "
"data, no lagged dependent variable: start with a plot of residuals over "
"time, the residuals' ACF/PACF, and Breusch-Godfrey; use Durbin-Watson only "
"as a quick lag-1 check. Regression with the lagged dependent variable or "
"other dynamic components: use Breusch-Godfrey and the residuals' ACF/PACF; "
"avoid relying on Durbin-Watson. Already-fitted ARIMA or SARIMA model: use "
"the residuals' ACF and Ljung-Box, to verify that the model has absorbed the "
"temporal dependence. General randomness without assuming a specific linear "
"relationship: use the Runs test, a sequence plot, and the ACF as a "
"complement.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Final "
"recommendation if a default test is needed:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em "
"0;\">General temporal or econometric regression: Breusch-Godfrey. "
"ARIMA/SARIMA or forecasting model: Ljung-Box. Quick, basic AR(1) "
"autocorrelation check in a static regression: Durbin-Watson. Nonparametric "
"check of randomness or sign pattern: Runs test. In almost every case, it is "
"worth accompanying the test with a residuals ACF: the p-value indicates "
"whether there is overall evidence against the absence of dependence, and "
"the ACF helps identify at which lags the problem lies.</p><p style=\"margin: "
"0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Preconditions:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 "
"0 0.6em 0;\">These tests make more sense when there is a temporal, spatial, "
"or sequential order. In cross-sectional data with no natural order, they "
"should be interpreted with caution.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; "
"font-weight: 700;\">Interpretation:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">p "
"&lt; .05 suggests a statistically significant deviation from independence "
"or randomness of residuals. p &gt;= .05 indicates that the result is "
"compatible with approximate independence or randomness of residuals.</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Methodological "
"decision:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">If dependence is present, "
"consider mixed models, GLS, time-series models, or HAC/Newey-West robust "
"errors, depending on the design.</p></div>"
msgstr ""

#: assumptionLibrary/results/independenceIrrelevantAlternativesGuide2.content
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box; "
"text-align: justify;\"><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">Hausman-McFadden — what it evaluates and its limitations:</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Tests H0: the coefficients shared between the "
"full model and a model refit without one category do not change beyond "
"sampling error (IIA holds), against H1: they do change (Hausman &amp; "
"McFadden, 1984). It is computed once per omitted category. In finite "
"samples, the statistic depends on the difference between two covariance "
"matrices, which is not always invertible — a well-documented numerical "
"degeneracy of this specific test, not evidence of a calculation error. When "
"this happens, the result is reported as \"not computable\": this is not "
"itself proof that IIA holds (Cheng &amp; Long, 2007, document that "
"Hausman-type IIA tests behave erratically in practice).</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Nested logit model — "
"what it does and when to use it:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Groups "
"the outcome's categories into a hierarchical nest structure, allowing "
"correlated unobserved factors within a nest while keeping independence "
"across nests. It is the more principled alternative when the categories "
"flagged by Hausman-McFadden share an identifiable substantive "
"grouping.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">Multinomial probit model — what it does and when to use it:</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Drops the logit's independent-errors "
"assumption altogether in favor of correlated, multivariate-normal errors "
"across alternatives. It is the more general - but computationally heavier - "
"alternative when no natural nest structure exists.</p><p style=\"margin: "
"0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Reporting coefficients with caution — "
"what it does and when to use it:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Rather "
"than switching models, the categories flagged by the Hausman-McFadden test "
"are documented explicitly and their odds ratios interpreted with added "
"reservation. It is a transparent option when only one or two categories are "
"flagged, not a widespread pattern.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; "
"font-weight: 700;\">Interpretation:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">p "
"&lt; .05 on the Hausman-McFadden test for an omitted category suggests a "
"statistically significant deviation from IIA for that category. p &gt;= .05 "
"indicates the result is compatible with IIA. A \"not computable\" result "
"should not be read as evidence either for or against IIA: it is a known "
"limitation of the test itself, not a substantive finding.</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Methodological "
"decision:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">If at least one omitted "
"category produces a significant statistic, consider a nested-logit or "
"multinomial-probit model (neither requires IIA) before relying on this "
"model's odds ratios, or document the flagged categories with explicit "
"caution. If no category is significant - or the result is mostly \"not "
"computable\" - this is compatible with (but not definitive proof of) IIA "
"holding for this model.</p></div>"
msgstr ""

#: assumptionLibrary/results/influenceGuide2.content
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box; "
"text-align: justify;\"><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">IQR — what it assesses and when to use it:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 "
"0.6em 0;\">Flags values below Q1 - 1.5*IQR or above Q3 + 1.5*IQR of the "
"variable being analyzed. In related groups this is applied to the paired "
"differences, not the original measurements. It is the simplest check and "
"does not depend on any fitted model — which makes it a natural starting "
"point before fitting any analysis.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; "
"font-weight: 700;\">Studentized residual — what it assesses and when to use "
"it:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Identifies observations with a large "
"discrepancy from what the model predicts, expressed in standard-error "
"units. It is useful for detecting cases the model fits poorly, but a large "
"residual does not automatically mean that case is moving the estimates — "
"for that, Cook's D or DFFITS are needed.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 "
"0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Leverage — what it assesses and a caution "
"about the threshold:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Identifies unusual "
"combinations of predictor values, without yet looking at the dependent "
"variable. Rule of thumb: leverage &gt; 2p/n, where p is the model's true "
"number of estimated parameters (length(coef(fit)) in R) — not the number of "
"selected variables. When a predictor is a factor with more than 2 levels, "
"each additional level adds a parameter through dummy coding; using the "
"variable count instead of the true parameter count understates the "
"threshold and flags too many cases.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; "
"font-weight: 700;\">Cook's D — what it assesses and when to use it:</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Combines leverage and residual magnitude into "
"a single measure of influence on the full set of estimated coefficients "
"(Cook, 1977). Rule of thumb: Cook's D &gt; 4/n. Treat it as a screening "
"threshold, not as confirmed influence: 4/n is numerically larger (more "
"lenient) the smaller the sample, and smaller (stricter) as it grows; even "
"so, small samples tend to keep flagging several cases because individual "
"Cook's D values tend to inflate when n is small.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em "
"0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">DFFITS — what it assesses and when to use "
"it:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Evaluates how much a case's own "
"fitted value changes when it is removed and the model is refit without it "
"(Belsley et al., 1980). Rule of thumb: |DFFITS| &gt; 2*sqrt(p/n). It is "
"conceptually very close to Cook's D — both capture influence on the fit — "
"so it is best read as corroborating evidence, not as an independent second "
"finding.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">Mahalanobis D² — what it assesses and when to use it:</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Evaluates a case's multivariate distance from "
"the predictor centroid, accounting for correlations among predictors "
"(Mahalanobis, 1936). It is the multivariate analogue of leverage: it "
"detects unusual predictor combinations even when no single variable is an "
"extreme value on its own. Like leverage, it says nothing by itself about "
"whether that unusual combination actually affects the results.</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Interpretation:</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">A flagged case should not be removed "
"automatically. It may be a data-entry error, a valid extreme case, or "
"important substantive evidence that the model is not capturing well.</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Methodological "
"decision:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Review the original data "
"point, run a sensitivity analysis (compare results with and without the "
"case), and justify any exclusion with transparent criteria declared in "
"advance — never chosen after seeing that the exclusion \"improves\" the "
"result.</p></div>"
msgstr ""

#: assumptionLibrary/results/homoscedasticityGuide2.content
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box; "
"text-align: justify;\"><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">Levene — what it assesses and when to use it:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 "
"0 0.6em 0;\">Compares dispersion across groups using each observation's "
"absolute deviation from its group mean, then applies a one-way ANOVA to "
"those deviations (Levene, 1960). H0: the k population variances are equal. "
"H1: at least one differs. It is a reasonable first check, especially with "
"large samples and approximately symmetric data.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em "
"0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Brown-Forsythe — what it assesses and when "
"to use it:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Follows the same logic as "
"Levene but centers each group on its median instead of its mean (Brown "
"&amp; Forsythe, 1974). Using the median makes it notably more robust to "
"skewness, heavy tails, and outliers. In practice, when there is doubt about "
"the shape of the distribution within each group, Brown-Forsythe should be "
"preferred over Levene by default.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; "
"font-weight: 700;\">Bartlett — what it assesses and when to use it:</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Tests equality of variances using a "
"likelihood-ratio approach that assumes normality within each group "
"(Bartlett, 1937). It is the most powerful test in this family when that "
"normality is reasonable, but it loses validity quickly if it is not: a "
"deviation from normality can make Bartlett reject equal variances even when "
"the true variances are equal. It should not be used as the sole basis for a "
"decision if the data are not normal — check the Normality section of this "
"library first.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">Fligner-Killeen — what it assesses and when to use it:</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">A nonparametric, rank-based test on the "
"absolute deviations from the median (Fligner &amp; Killeen, 1976). It makes "
"no distributional assumption, which makes it the most robust option in this "
"family against non-normality and outliers, at the cost of some power when "
"the data are in fact reasonably normal.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em "
"0; font-weight: 700;\">Hartley's Fmax — what it assesses and "
"limitations:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Simply the ratio between "
"the largest and smallest sample variance among the k groups (Hartley, "
"1950). It is easy to compute and interpret, but its tabulated critical "
"values are only strictly valid with balanced designs (equal n per group) "
"and normality; it is therefore used more as a complementary descriptive "
"indicator than as a primary formal test.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 "
"0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Interpretation (group tests):</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">p &lt; .05 suggests statistically significant "
"differences between group variances. p &gt;= .05 indicates that the result "
"is compatible with approximately homogeneous variances.</p></div>"
msgstr ""

#: assumptionLibrary/results/sphericityGuide2.content
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box; "
"text-align: justify;\"><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">Mauchly — what it assesses and limitations:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 "
"0.6em 0;\">Tests H0: the variances of the differences between all pairs of "
"levels are equal (sphericity), against H1: at least one pair differs "
"(Mauchly, 1940). It is the historical reference test, but its behavior is "
"not uniform: with small samples it can lack power to detect real "
"violations, and with large samples it can flag deviations from sphericity "
"too small to have practical relevance for the F statistic.</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Greenhouse-Geisser — "
"what it does and when to use it:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Rather "
"than testing sphericity directly, it estimates how far the observed "
"covariance pattern departs from it (epsilon, between 1/(k-1) and 1) and "
"reduces the F test's degrees of freedom by multiplying them by that epsilon "
"(Greenhouse &amp; Geisser, 1959). The F statistic itself does not change; "
"only the degrees of freedom used to obtain the p-value change, making the "
"test more conservative. It is the recommended default correction when the "
"estimated epsilon is low (&lt; .75).</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; "
"font-weight: 700;\">Huynh-Feldt — what it does and when to use it:</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">An alternative correction that adjusts the "
"Greenhouse-Geisser epsilon to reduce its tendency to be overly "
"conservative, especially when the sphericity violation is mild or moderate "
"(Huynh &amp; Feldt, 1976). The usual convention is to prefer Huynh-Feldt "
"when the Greenhouse-Geisser-estimated epsilon exceeds .75, and "
"Greenhouse-Geisser when it is below that.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 "
"0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Lower-bound — what it does and when to use "
"it:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Applies the most extreme "
"mathematically possible correction, assuming the worst-case violation of "
"sphericity. It is useful as a safety bound — if the effect remains "
"significant even under this severe correction, the conclusion is robust to "
"any degree of sphericity violation — but it sacrifices considerable power "
"and is rarely the final correction reported.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 "
"0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Interpretation:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em "
"0;\">p &lt; .05 on Mauchly's test suggests a statistically significant "
"deviation from sphericity. p &gt;= .05 indicates that the result is "
"compatible with approximate sphericity.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em "
"0; font-weight: 700;\">Methodological decision:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 "
"0.6em 0;\">If there is evidence against sphericity, use the "
"Greenhouse-Geisser or Huynh-Feldt correction depending on the estimated "
"epsilon, consider the nonparametric Friedman test (Friedman, 1937) as an "
"alternative, or fit a mixed model that does not assume sphericity, "
"depending on the study design.</p></div>"
msgstr ""

#: assumptionLibrary/results/normalityGuide2.content
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box; "
"text-align: justify;\"><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">Shapiro-Wilk:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">A classic and widely "
"used normality test (Shapiro &amp; Wilk, 1965). Requires at least 3 valid "
"cases. In R it is computed for up to 5000 observations. It is especially "
"powerful in small to moderate samples relative to the other tests in this "
"battery (Razali &amp; Wah, 2011).</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; "
"font-weight: 700;\">Lilliefors (corrected Kolmogorov-Smirnov):</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Compares the observed distribution against a "
"theoretical one, but corrects the critical values because the mean and "
"standard deviation are estimated from the same data (Lilliefors, 1967). The "
"classic Kolmogorov-Smirnov test (without this correction) assumes "
"parameters known in advance and is not valid in this context; this is why "
"this module always uses the corrected Lilliefors version, not the classic "
"K-S test.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">Anderson-Darling:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Gives more "
"weight to the tails (Anderson &amp; Darling, 1952). It is useful when heavy "
"tails, extreme values, or deviations at the ends of the distribution are a "
"concern.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">Cramér-von Mises:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Compares the "
"entire observed cumulative distribution against the theoretical one "
"(Anderson &amp; Darling, 1952). It is more sensitive than "
"Kolmogorov-Smirnov to deviations spread across the central body of the "
"distribution, not just at one point.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; "
"font-weight: 700;\">Shapiro-Francia:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">A "
"variant of Shapiro-Wilk based on the same principles, with a different "
"calculation that can perform better in some moderate or large samples "
"(Shapiro &amp; Francia, 1972).</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; "
"font-weight: 700;\">Pearson chi-square:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em "
"0;\">Compares observed and expected frequencies by interval. It depends on "
"how the data are grouped into intervals, so it tends to be less powerful "
"than tests based on the full distribution.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 "
"0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Jarque-Bera:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em "
"0;\">Jointly evaluates skewness and kurtosis (Jarque &amp; Bera, 1987). It "
"is more informative in moderate or large samples, and less stable in very "
"small samples.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">Skewness test:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Evaluates skewness. "
"Positive skewness suggests a long right tail; negative skewness suggests a "
"long left tail.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">Kurtosis test:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Evaluates "
"peakedness and tail weight. It can indicate heavy tails, light tails, or a "
"concentration different from what is expected under normality.</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Sample-size "
"criteria:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.2em 1.2em;\"><b>n &lt; 10:</b> "
"interpretation is very unstable.</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.2em "
"1.2em;\"><b>10 &lt;= n &lt; 30:</b> small sample; low power to detect "
"deviations.</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.2em 1.2em;\"><b>30 &lt;= n &lt;= "
"200:</b> moderate sample; the tests are useful diagnostics.</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0 0 0.2em 1.2em;\"><b>n &gt; 200:</b> small deviations can "
"produce significant p-values.</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.2em 1.2em;\"><b>n "
"&gt; 5000:</b> Shapiro-Wilk is not computed in R.</p><p style=\"margin: "
"0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Interpretation:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 "
"0 0.6em 0;\">p &lt; .05 suggests a statistically significant deviation from "
"a normal distribution. p &gt;= .05 indicates that the result is compatible "
"with an approximately normal distribution.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 "
"0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Methodological decision:</p><p style=\"margin: "
"0 0 0.6em 0;\">Combine tests, plots, skewness, kurtosis, sample size, and "
"outliers. If several tests suggest non-normality, consider transformation, "
"bootstrap, robust methods, or nonparametric alternatives.</p></div>"
msgstr ""

#: assumptionLibrary/results/robustGuide2.content
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box; "
"text-align: justify;\"><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">Transformations of the dependent variable:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 "
"0.6em 0;\">log(Y): useful with marked positive skewness and values strictly "
"greater than 0. sqrt(Y): useful with count data or mild-to-moderate "
"positive skewness, and values &gt;= 0. 1/Y: useful only with caution for "
"severe positive skewness and values not close to zero (the inverse "
"transformation is highly sensitive near zero). Every transformation changes "
"the scale on which results are interpreted, so this should be reported "
"explicitly.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Welch "
"— what it does and when to use it:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em "
"0;\">Adjusts the degrees of freedom of the t or F statistic so that equal "
"variances across groups are not required (Welch, 1947). It is especially "
"recommended when group sizes are unequal, because in that scenario the "
"classic t-test or ANOVA is more sensitive to unequal variances than when "
"groups are similarly sized.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; "
"font-weight: 700;\">Brown-Forsythe (as a robust ANOVA alternative):</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Applies the same logic as Welch but using "
"location estimators more robust than the mean, which makes it preferable "
"when, in addition to unequal variances, there is skewness or outliers in "
"the groups (Brown &amp; Forsythe, 1974).</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 "
"0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Wilcoxon signed-rank — what it does and when "
"to use it:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">A nonparametric alternative "
"for two related measurements: instead of comparing the paired differences "
"directly, it converts them to ranks and tests whether the sum of positively "
"signed ranks differs from what is expected under H0 of symmetry around zero "
"(Wilcoxon, 1945). It requires the differences to be approximately "
"symmetric, though not normal; with strong asymmetry in the differences, "
"this test also loses validity.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; "
"font-weight: 700;\">Friedman — what it does and when to use it:</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Extends the same rank-based logic to three or "
"more related measurements, converting each row (subject) into ranks across "
"conditions and testing whether those ranks differ systematically across "
"conditions (Friedman, 1937). It is the natural nonparametric alternative to "
"repeated-measures ANOVA when normality of the differences is not "
"defensible.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">HC3 "
"robust errors — what they do and when to use them:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 "
"0.6em 0;\">Recompute a regression's coefficient standard errors so they "
"remain valid even under heteroscedasticity, without changing the "
"coefficients' point estimates (MacKinnon &amp; White, 1985). HC3 is the "
"recommended variant in small or moderate samples, because it corrects the "
"bias in variance estimates better than other HC variants in that "
"regime.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Mixed "
"models:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Appropriate for nested, "
"longitudinal, repeated, or otherwise dependence-structured data that a "
"simple fixed-effects model cannot represent. Rather than correcting a "
"violated assumption after the fact, they directly model the source of "
"dependence (for example, variation across subjects or across groups) as "
"part of the model structure.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; "
"font-weight: 700;\">Methodological decision:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em "
"0;\">Do not switch methods based on a single isolated test. The alternative "
"chosen should respond to the overall pattern of evidence, the study design, "
"and the research question — not just the p-value of one "
"diagnostic.</p></div>"
msgstr ""

#: assumptionLibrary/results/multicollinearityGuide2.content
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box; "
"text-align: justify;\"><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">VIF and tolerance — what they assess:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em "
"0;\">A predictor's VIF is obtained by regressing that predictor on all the "
"other predictors in the model and using the resulting R²: VIF = 1/(1-R²). A "
"high VIF means much of that predictor's variation can already be explained "
"by the others, leaving little \"own\" information to estimate its coefficient "
"precisely. Tolerance is simply 1/VIF, on the opposite scale.</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">VIF and tolerance — "
"when to use them and a caution about thresholds:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 "
"0.6em 0;\">They are the most common and easiest-to-interpret per-variable "
"diagnostic. The conventional thresholds (VIF &lt; 5 or &lt; 10) are widely "
"used rules, not universal limits derived mathematically: the tolerable "
"level of VIF depends on sample size, how much variance the model explains, "
"and whether the interest is in individual coefficients or only in overall "
"prediction (O'Brien, 2007). With large samples, moderate VIFs can be "
"harmless; with small samples, even VIFs of 3-4 can already produce unstable "
"coefficients.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">Maximum correlation between predictors:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 "
"0.6em 0;\">The simplest bivariate check: it identifies pairs of predictors "
"that are highly correlated with each other. It is useful as a first glance, "
"but it does not detect \"distributed\" collinearity, where no single pair is "
"highly correlated but a linear combination of three or more predictors is — "
"that kind of collinearity only shows up in VIF, eigenvalues, or the "
"condition index.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">Condition index and eigenvalues:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em "
"0;\">These come from the decomposition of the standardized predictor matrix. "
"An eigenvalue near zero indicates a direction in predictor space with "
"almost no independent variation — that is, a nearly redundant linear "
"combination. The condition index summarizes this as the ratio between the "
"largest and smallest eigenvalue; high values flag collinearity, but it is "
"worth also reviewing the variance proportions by dimension to identify "
"which specific predictors are involved (Belsley et al., 1980).</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Determinant and model "
"rank:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">A near-zero determinant of the "
"predictor correlation matrix is another aggregate signal of redundancy. "
"Model rank is a different and stricter check: if the rank is less than the "
"number of columns in the design matrix, an exact linear dependency exists "
"(not just high correlation), and some model parameters are not estimable at "
"all — R typically flags them as NA in the model output.</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Methodological "
"decision:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">If collinearity is high but "
"the main goal is prediction, it may not require action. If the goal is "
"interpreting individual coefficients, consider removing or combining "
"redundant predictors, centering polynomial terms and interactions (which "
"reduces structural collinearity without changing the substantive model), or "
"using penalized methods (ridge, lasso) that stabilize estimates in exchange "
"for controlled bias (Marquardt, 1970).</p></div>"
msgstr ""

#: assumptionLibrary/results/independence.content
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box; "
"text-align: justify;\"><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">What it assesses:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Evaluates "
"whether observations or errors can be considered independent. This "
"assumption depends mainly on the study design.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 "
"0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Where it's used in AssumptionsLab:</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0 0 0.2em 1.2em;\"><b>ANOVA/ANCOVA:</b> independence between "
"units or subjects.</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.2em "
"1.2em;\"><b>Regression:</b> independence of errors, especially in ordered "
"data.</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.2em 1.2em;\"><b>Related Groups:</b> "
"dependence within a unit is expected, independence between units.</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Core idea:</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">The four tests in this section relate to the "
"temporal dependence of residuals, but they do not answer exactly the same "
"question or apply to the same object. In a regression or time series, one "
"usually wants the residuals to be approximately white noise, i.e., with no "
"systematic temporal pattern. If there is residual autocorrelation, the OLS "
"coefficients can remain unbiased under certain conditions, but the standard "
"errors, t-tests, confidence intervals, and F-tests can become "
"unreliable.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">The "
"choice among the four depends mainly on:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em "
"0;\">Whether you are analyzing residuals from a regression or residuals from "
"an ARIMA/SARIMA-type time-series model. Whether autocorrelation is "
"suspected only at lag 1 or at several lags at once. Whether the model "
"contains the lagged dependent variable as a regressor. Whether the goal is "
"to detect linear autocorrelation specifically, or a more general lack of "
"randomness in the sequence.</p></div>"
msgstr ""

#: assumptionLibrary/results/influence.content
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box; "
"text-align: justify;\"><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">What it assesses:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Evaluates "
"whether results may be dominated by a few extreme, unusual, or highly "
"influential cases rather than reflecting the general pattern of the "
"data.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Where it's "
"used in AssumptionsLab:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Independent "
"Groups, Related Groups, ANOVA/ANCOVA, and Regression.</p><p style=\"margin: "
"0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">An important distinction:</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">An outlier is a value unusual relative to the "
"rest of the data; an influential case is one whose presence or absence "
"substantially changes the analysis's result. A case can be an outlier "
"without being influential, and influential without being an obvious outlier "
"— this is why several diagnostics are needed, not just one (Belsley et al., "
"1980; Cook, 1977).</p></div>"
msgstr ""

#: assumptionLibrary/results/multicollinearity.content
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box; "
"text-align: justify;\"><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">What it assesses:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Evaluates "
"whether the model's predictors are too closely related to one another. "
"Multicollinearity does not necessarily reduce the model's overall "
"predictive power, but it inflates the variance of individual coefficients "
"and makes interpreting them separately harder (Marquardt, 1970).</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Where it's used in "
"AssumptionsLab:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Regression and linear "
"models with several predictors.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; "
"font-weight: 700;\">Why it matters:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">When "
"two or more predictors share a lot of information, the model cannot "
"precisely separate each one's own effect; individual coefficients become "
"unstable (they change substantially when cases or predictors are added or "
"removed) and their standard errors inflate, even though the model's overall "
"fit (R²) and its predictions can remain reasonable (O'Brien, "
"2007).</p></div>"
msgstr ""

#: assumptionLibrary/results/linearity.content
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box; "
"text-align: justify;\"><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">What it assesses:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Evaluates "
"whether the relationship between numeric predictors or covariates and the "
"dependent variable can reasonably be represented by a straight line.</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Where it's used in "
"AssumptionsLab:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.2em 1.2em;\"><b>Regression:</b> "
"numeric predictors and overall model fit.</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.2em "
"1.2em;\"><b>ANOVA/ANCOVA:</b> numeric covariates.</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 "
"0.2em 1.2em;\"><b>Path/Related/Logistic:</b> dCor and copula entropy as a "
"non-linear dependence diagnostic complementary to Pearson.</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">It does not apply the same way to categorical "
"factors, because these represent groups or levels, not continuous linear "
"relationships.</p></div>"
msgstr ""

#: assumptionLibrary/results/homoscedasticity.content
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box; "
"text-align: justify;\"><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">What it assesses:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Evaluates "
"whether variability is approximately constant. In group comparisons this is "
"called homogeneity of variance (Levene, 1960); in regression it is called "
"homoscedasticity of residuals.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; "
"font-weight: 700;\">Where it's used in AssumptionsLab:</p><p style=\"margin: "
"0 0 0.2em 1.2em;\"><b>Independent Groups:</b> equality of variances across "
"groups.</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.2em 1.2em;\"><b>ANOVA/ANCOVA:</b> "
"equality of variances across cells or groups.</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 "
"0.2em 1.2em;\"><b>Regression:</b> constant residual variance across fitted "
"values.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Why it "
"matters:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">When group variances are very "
"different, the standard error of the test statistic no longer correctly "
"reflects the real variability, and confidence intervals and p-values can "
"become unreliable — especially with unequal group sizes (Welch, 1947). In "
"regression, heteroscedasticity does not bias OLS coefficients, but it does "
"bias their standard errors, affecting t and F tests on those "
"coefficients.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Two "
"families of tests:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Group tests (Levene, "
"Brown-Forsythe, Bartlett, Fligner-Killeen, Hartley's Fmax) compare "
"dispersion across k predefined groups. Regression tests (Breusch-Pagan, "
"White, Goldfeld-Quandt, Spearman between residuals and fitted values) "
"evaluate whether residual variance changes systematically with predicted "
"values or predictors, rather than comparing discrete groups.</p></div>"
msgstr ""

#: assumptionLibrary/results/normality.content
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box; "
"text-align: justify;\"><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">What it assesses:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Normality "
"evaluates whether a distribution reasonably approximates a normal "
"distribution. In applied statistical analysis, it is not always the "
"original variable that is evaluated.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; "
"font-weight: 700;\">Where it's used in AssumptionsLab:</p><p style=\"margin: "
"0 0 0.2em 1.2em;\"><b>Independent Groups:</b> normality by group or of "
"residuals, depending on context.</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.2em "
"1.2em;\"><b>Related Groups:</b> normality of paired differences or "
"within-subject residuals.</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.2em "
"1.2em;\"><b>ANOVA/ANCOVA:</b> normality of model residuals.</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0 0 0.2em 1.2em;\"><b>Regression:</b> normality of model "
"residuals.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">How "
"the battery is interpreted:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Shapiro-Wilk "
"is used as the primary test because it is the most powerful in small to "
"moderate samples; the others are reported as secondary evidence. The "
"interpretation integrates both: how many of the secondary tests agree with "
"Shapiro-Wilk's conclusion, not just whether Shapiro-Wilk alone is "
"significant.</p></div>"
msgstr ""

#: assumptionLibrary/results/sphericity.content
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box; "
"text-align: justify;\"><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">What it assesses:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Sphericity "
"evaluates whether the variances of the differences between all possible "
"pairs of repeated measurements are approximately equal (Mauchly, "
"1940).</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Where it's "
"used in AssumptionsLab:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">Related Groups "
"with three or more related measurements.</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em "
"0;\">It does not apply with only two measurements, because with two levels "
"there is only one possible pair of differences, and no variances to compare "
"against each other.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">Why it matters:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">The classic "
"repeated-measures ANOVA assumes sphericity so that its F statistic follows "
"the nominal F distribution. When it is violated, the observed F tends to "
"inflate and the real Type I error exceeds the nominal one; the "
"degrees-of-freedom corrections exist precisely to compensate for that "
"inflation without changing the F statistic itself.</p></div>"
msgstr ""

#: assumptionLibrary/results/independenceIrrelevantAlternatives.content
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box; "
"text-align: justify;\"><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">What it evaluates:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">The "
"multinomial-logit model assumes the relative odds between any two "
"categories of the outcome do not depend on which other categories are also "
"available in the choice set - Independence of Irrelevant Alternatives "
"(IIA). It is the one assumption unique to this model family, with no "
"equivalent in binary or ordinal logistic regression.</p><p style=\"margin: "
"0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Where it is used in "
"AssumptionsLab:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">multCheck (multinomial "
"logistic regression).</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">It does not apply "
"to binary logistic regression (logCheck), since with only two categories "
"there are no \"irrelevant alternatives\" that could be dropped from the "
"choice set. It also does not apply to ordinal logistic regression "
"(ordCheck), which instead depends on the proportional-odds (parallel-lines) "
"assumption across ordered cutpoints.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; "
"font-weight: 700;\">Why it matters:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">When "
"violated, the reported odds ratios between categories are not stable - they "
"shift depending on which other categories are present in the choice set - "
"which undermines interpreting a single set of coefficients as describing a "
"consistent preference among alternatives.</p></div>"
msgstr ""

#: assumptionLibrary/results/proportionalOdds.content
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box; "
"text-align: justify;\"><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">What it evaluates:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">The "
"proportional-odds model assumes each predictor's effect on the ordinal "
"outcome has the same magnitude at every cutpoint between categories (the "
"\"parallel lines\" assumption). Brant's (1990) test is the standard "
"check.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">Where it "
"is used in AssumptionsLab:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">ordCheck "
"(ordinal logistic regression).</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">It does "
"not apply to binary logistic regression (logCheck) or multinomial "
"regression (multCheck), since neither has multiple cutpoints whose slopes "
"need to be compared against each other.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em "
"0; font-weight: 700;\">Why it matters:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em "
"0;\">When violated, a single reported odds ratio per predictor misrepresents "
"its real effect - understating it at some cutpoints and overstating it at "
"others - and can lead to mistaken substantive conclusions about which "
"categories a predictor actually discriminates between.</p></div>"
msgstr ""

#: assumptionLibrary/results/robust.content
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box; "
"text-align: justify;\"><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">What they assess:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">These are not "
"assumptions in themselves, but methodological responses when a specific "
"assumption is not defensible or when results are sensitive to important "
"deviations from it.</p><p style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: "
"700;\">Where they're used in AssumptionsLab:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em "
"0;\">Independent Groups, Related Groups, ANOVA/ANCOVA, and Regression.</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0.7em 0 0.15em 0; font-weight: 700;\">How to choose among "
"them:</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.6em 0;\">The right alternative depends on "
"which specific assumption fails, not on a general preference for \"robust\" "
"methods. Switching methods without first identifying which assumption was "
"violated tends to complicate interpretation without solving the real "
"problem.</p></div>"
msgstr ""

#: R/pathcheck.b.R
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box;\"><p "
"style=\"font-weight: 700; margin: 0 0 0.10em 0; line-height: "
"1.25;\">AssumptionsLab</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.35em 0; line-height: "
"1.25;\">Assumption check for path analysis</p><p style=\"margin: 0 0 0.25em "
"0;\"> </p><p style=\"margin: 0; line-height: 1.35;\">Use this analysis when "
"you want to review whether a path model has defensible methodological "
"assumptions. The goal is not only to compute tests, but to help justify the "
"statistical decision with evidence obtained from your own data.</p><p "
"style=\"margin: 0 0 0.25em 0;\"> </p></div>"
msgstr ""

#: assumptionLibrary/results/proportionalOddsTable.content
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box;\"><table "
"style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 0.5em 0 0.8em 0; "
"font-size: 0.95em;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px "
"8px; border-bottom: 2px solid #999; font-weight: 700;\">Diagnostic / "
"alternative</th><th style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 2px solid #999; font-weight: 700;\">What it does</th><th "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 2px solid #999; "
"font-weight: 700;\">When to use it</th><th style=\"text-align: left; padding: "
"4px 8px; border-bottom: 2px solid #999; font-weight: 700;\">Main "
"limitation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Brant test</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Tests whether each "
"predictor's coefficient is equal across every cutpoint</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">As an initial check right after fitting the model, "
"before interpreting a single OR per predictor</td><td style=\"text-align: "
"left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Limited power in small samples or sparse categories; a "
"non-significant result is weak evidence of proportionality</td></tr><tr><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Partial proportional-odds model</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Lets only the predictors flagged by Brant vary by "
"cutpoint, keeping the rest at a single coefficient</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">When Brant flags one or two specific predictors, not "
"the full omnibus</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Less standardized "
"across statistical software; interpretation gets more complex when mixing "
"constant and varying effects</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Multinomial model (multCheck)</td><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Abandons the proportionality assumption by treating each category as "
"nominal</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: "
"1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">When the violation is widespread "
"(significant omnibus, several predictors affected)</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Loses the efficiency of exploiting the categories' "
"order; more parameters to estimate</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: "
"left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Report cutpoint-specific effects</td><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Fits "
"separate binary logits at each cutpoint and reports a different OR for "
"each</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px "
"solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">As a transparent alternative when the "
"goal is documenting the pattern rather than forcing a single model</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">No single pooled model summarizes the effect; "
"comparisons across cutpoints lose some statistical "
"efficiency</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"
msgstr ""

#: assumptionLibrary/results/independenceIrrelevantAlternativesTable.content
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box;\"><table "
"style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 0.5em 0 0.8em 0; "
"font-size: 0.95em;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px "
"8px; border-bottom: 2px solid #999; font-weight: 700;\">Diagnostic / "
"alternative</th><th style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 2px solid #999; font-weight: 700;\">What it does</th><th "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 2px solid #999; "
"font-weight: 700;\">When to use it</th><th style=\"text-align: left; padding: "
"4px 8px; border-bottom: 2px solid #999; font-weight: 700;\">Main "
"limitation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Hausman-McFadden test</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px "
"8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Refits the model "
"omitting each outcome category in turn and compares the coefficients shared "
"with the full model</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">As an initial check "
"right after fitting the multinomial model, before relying on the reported "
"odds ratios</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">The statistic is not "
"always computable (the covariance-matrix difference can fail to be "
"invertible in finite samples); a non-computable result is not proof that "
"IIA holds</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Nested logit "
"model</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px "
"solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Groups outcome categories into a "
"hierarchical nest structure, allowing correlation between alternatives "
"within the same nest</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">When the IIA violation "
"follows a substantively meaningful grouping pattern (e.g., categories "
"sharing unobserved attributes)</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: "
"4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Requires "
"specifying a reasonable nest structure a priori; results are sensitive to "
"that choice</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Multinomial probit "
"model</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px "
"solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Replaces the logit's independent-errors "
"assumption with multivariate-normal errors, allowing correlation between "
"alternatives</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">When enough computing "
"time is available and no natural nest structure exists</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Computationally more expensive; has no closed form "
"and requires numerical integration or simulation</td></tr><tr><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Report coefficients with caution</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Instead of discarding the model, the categories "
"flagged by Hausman-McFadden are documented and their odds ratios "
"interpreted with added reservation</td><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">As a "
"transparent alternative when the violation affects only one or two specific "
"categories rather than the general pattern</td><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Does "
"not fix the underlying problem, only documents it, leaving interpretive "
"caution to the reader</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"
msgstr ""

#: assumptionLibrary/results/sphericityTable.content
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box;\"><table "
"style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 0.5em 0 0.8em 0; "
"font-size: 0.95em;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px "
"8px; border-bottom: 2px solid #999; font-weight: 700;\">Diagnostic / "
"correction</th><th style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 2px solid #999; font-weight: 700;\">What it does</th><th "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 2px solid #999; "
"font-weight: 700;\">When to use it</th><th style=\"text-align: left; padding: "
"4px 8px; border-bottom: 2px solid #999; font-weight: 700;\">Main "
"limitation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Mauchly (W)</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Tests the hypothesis "
"of exact sphericity</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">As an initial check "
"before deciding whether to correct degrees of freedom</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Unstable with small samples and overly sensitive "
"(flags trivial violations) with large samples</td></tr><tr><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Greenhouse-Geisser</td><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Corrects degrees of freedom by multiplying them by an estimated "
"epsilon</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: "
"1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Conservative default correction, "
"especially if epsilon &lt; .75</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: "
"4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Can be "
"excessively conservative (loses power) when the real violation is "
"mild</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Huynh-Feldt</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Corrects degrees of freedom with an adjusted epsilon, "
"less conservative than GG</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px "
"8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Preferred over "
"Greenhouse-Geisser when the estimated epsilon &gt; .75</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Can overestimate epsilon (be too liberal) in small "
"samples</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Lower-bound</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Applies the most severe possible correction (epsilon "
"= 1/(k-1))</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">As an extreme "
"conservative bound when avoiding false positives is the top "
"priority</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: "
"1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Very conservative: sacrifices "
"considerable statistical power relative to GG or "
"HF</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"
msgstr ""

#: assumptionLibrary/results/linearityTable.content
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box;\"><table "
"style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 0.5em 0 0.8em 0; "
"font-size: 0.95em;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px "
"8px; border-bottom: 2px solid #999; font-weight: 700;\">Diagnostic</th><th "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 2px solid #999; "
"font-weight: 700;\">What it assesses</th><th style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 2px solid #999; font-weight: 700;\">Typical "
"use</th><th style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 2px "
"solid #999; font-weight: 700;\">Main "
"limitation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Bivariate correlation</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px "
"8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Simple linear "
"association between two variables</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: "
"4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Initial "
"exploratory glance</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Only detects linear "
"association; does not replace diagnosing the full model</td></tr><tr><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">dCor / copula entropy</td><td style=\"text-align: "
"left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Linear and non-linear dependence, without assuming a functional "
"form</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px "
"solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Exploratory complement to Pearson when a "
"non-linear relationship is suspected</td><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">A "
"dCor-Pearson gap is a signal, not proof; needs confirming with a "
"scatterplot</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Quadratic term</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Curvature of a specific predictor against the "
"original model</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Quick check, one "
"predictor at a time</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Does not replace "
"RESET, which evaluates the full model</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: "
"left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Box-Tidwell</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Whether a specific "
"predictor needs a power transformation</td><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Reference test for logit-linearity (logistic regression)</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Evaluates one predictor at a time; requires x &gt; 0; "
"does not assess joint curvature (Box &amp; Tidwell, 1962)</td></tr><tr><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Ramsey RESET</td><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Overall functional misspecification of the model</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Global check after reviewing individual "
"predictors</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Indicates something is "
"wrong, but not what (curvature, omitted interaction, outlier) (Ramsey, "
"1969)</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Rainbow test</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Whether the linear fit is stable across the full "
"range of fitted values</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Complement to RESET "
"when non-linearity may be concentrated at the extremes</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Requires a reasonable sample (at least 20 cases in "
"this module) for the central sub-model</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"
msgstr ""

#: assumptionLibrary/results/influenceTable.content
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box;\"><table "
"style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 0.5em 0 0.8em 0; "
"font-size: 0.95em;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px "
"8px; border-bottom: 2px solid #999; font-weight: 700;\">Diagnostic</th><th "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 2px solid #999; "
"font-weight: 700;\">What it measures</th><th style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 2px solid #999; font-weight: 700;\">Common "
"rule of thumb</th><th style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 2px solid #999; font-weight: 700;\">Main "
"limitation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">IQR</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: "
"1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Distance of a value from the "
"interquartile range</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Outside Q1 − 1.5·IQR "
"or Q3 + 1.5·IQR</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Looks only at the "
"variable univariately; does not consider the model or other "
"variables</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Studentized "
"residual</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: "
"1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">The case's discrepancy from the "
"fitted model, in standardized error units</td><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">|studentized residual| &gt; 3</td><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">A "
"large residual flags poor local fit, not necessarily that the case moves "
"the estimates</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Leverage</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">How unusual the case's combination of predictors "
"is</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px "
"solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Leverage &gt; 2p/n (p = true number of "
"estimated parameters)</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">High leverage alone "
"does not imply the case is influential if its residual is "
"small</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Cook's D</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">The case's joint influence on all of the model's "
"estimates</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: "
"1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Cook's D &gt; 4/n, as a screening "
"threshold (Cook, 1977)</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">4/n is more lenient "
"with small samples and stricter with large ones; treat as screening, not "
"confirmation</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">DFFITS</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">How much the case's own fitted value changes when it "
"is excluded from the model</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px "
"8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">|DFFITS| &gt; "
"2·√(p/n)</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: "
"1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Sensitive to the same kind of cases "
"as Cook's D; best read together, not separately</td></tr><tr><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Mahalanobis D²</td><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">The "
"case's multivariate distance from the predictor centroid</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Large values relative to a chi-square with p degrees "
"of freedom</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Detects unusual "
"predictor combinations (multivariate leverage), not whether those "
"combinations affect the result (Mahalanobis, "
"1936)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"
msgstr ""

#: assumptionLibrary/results/multicollinearityTable.content
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box;\"><table "
"style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 0.5em 0 0.8em 0; "
"font-size: 0.95em;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px "
"8px; border-bottom: 2px solid #999; font-weight: 700;\">Diagnostic</th><th "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 2px solid #999; "
"font-weight: 700;\">What it measures</th><th style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 2px solid #999; font-weight: 700;\">Common "
"rule of thumb</th><th style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 2px solid #999; font-weight: 700;\">Main "
"limitation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">VIF</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: "
"1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">How much a coefficient's variance is "
"inflated by its association with the other predictors</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">VIF &lt; 5 acceptable; 5-10 concern; ≥ 10 severe "
"problem</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: "
"1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">The thresholds are conventions, not "
"universal rules (O'Brien, 2007); does not identify which variables are "
"redundant with which</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px "
"8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Tolerance</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">The inverse of VIF (1/VIF)</td><td style=\"text-align: "
"left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">&gt; .20 acceptable; &lt; .20 concern; &lt; .10 severe "
"problem</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: "
"1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Same information as VIF on a "
"different scale; adds nothing new by itself</td></tr><tr><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Maximum correlation</td><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">The "
"largest bivariate correlation between pairs of predictors</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">|r| ≥ .80 suggests possible bivariate "
"redundancy</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Only detects "
"collinearity between pairs; does not see redundancy spread across three or "
"more predictors</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Condition "
"index</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px "
"solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">The ratio between the largest and "
"smallest eigenvalue of the standardized predictor matrix</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">&gt; 15 concern; &gt; 30 severe collinearity</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Requires also reviewing variance proportions by "
"dimension to know which predictors are involved</td></tr><tr><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Eigenvalues / determinant</td><td style=\"text-align: "
"left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">How close the predictor matrix is to being singular</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Values near zero suggest redundant dimensions</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">An aggregate signal; does not directly identify which "
"combination of predictors is redundant</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: "
"left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Model rank</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Whether an exact "
"linear dependency exists among predictors</td><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Rank "
"&lt; number of columns indicates non-estimable parameters</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">An exact-identification problem, distinct from the "
"approximate collinearity the measures above "
"capture</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"
msgstr ""

#: assumptionLibrary/results/robustTable.content
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box;\"><table "
"style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 0.5em 0 0.8em 0; "
"font-size: 0.95em;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px "
"8px; border-bottom: 2px solid #999; font-weight: 700;\">If the problem "
"is...</th><th style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 2px "
"solid #999; font-weight: 700;\">Common alternative</th><th "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 2px solid #999; "
"font-weight: 700;\">Applies in</th><th style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px "
"8px; border-bottom: 2px solid #999; font-weight: 700;\">Core "
"idea</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px "
"8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Unequal variances "
"across groups</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Welch "
"correction</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Independent Groups, "
"ANOVA</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px "
"solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Adjusts the degrees of freedom of the t "
"or F statistic without assuming equal variances (Welch, "
"1947)</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Unequal variances, "
"with skewness or outliers</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px "
"8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Brown-Forsythe "
"(as robust ANOVA)</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">ANOVA</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Extends Welch's logic using location estimators more "
"robust than the mean</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px "
"8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Non-normal but "
"symmetric paired differences</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px "
"8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Wilcoxon "
"signed-rank</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Related Groups (two "
"measurements)</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Uses the ranks of the "
"differences instead of their values, without assuming normality (Wilcoxon, "
"1945)</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Three or more related "
"measurements, non-normal</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Friedman</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Related Groups (three or more measurements)</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Extends the rank-based logic to more than two related "
"measurements (Friedman, 1937)</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Heteroscedastic residuals</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: "
"4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">HC3 robust "
"standard errors</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Regression</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Corrects the standard errors of coefficients without "
"changing their point estimates (MacKinnon &amp; White, "
"1985)</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Marked skewness of the "
"dependent variable</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Transformation (log, "
"root, inverse)</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Any analysis with a "
"continuous variable</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Changes the variable's "
"scale so its distribution more closely approaches "
"normality</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Complex dependence "
"structure (nested, longitudinal)</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: "
"4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Mixed "
"models</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: "
"1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Repeated, nested, or hierarchical "
"data</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px "
"solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Explicitly models the correlation between "
"related observations instead of assuming "
"independence</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"
msgstr ""

#: assumptionLibrary/results/homoscedasticityRegTable.content
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box;\"><table "
"style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 0.5em 0 0.8em 0; "
"font-size: 0.95em;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px "
"8px; border-bottom: 2px solid #999; font-weight: 700;\">Test</th><th "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 2px solid #999; "
"font-weight: 700;\">What it tests</th><th style=\"text-align: left; padding: "
"4px 8px; border-bottom: 2px solid #999; font-weight: 700;\">Typical "
"use</th><th style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 2px "
"solid #999; font-weight: 700;\">Main "
"limitation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Breusch-Pagan</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Whether residual "
"variance depends linearly on predictors or fitted values</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Standard heteroscedasticity check in linear "
"regression</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Assumes the shape of "
"any heteroscedasticity is approximately linear in the "
"predictors</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">White</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">General heteroscedasticity, including non-linear and "
"interaction patterns</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">When a more complex "
"form of heteroscedasticity than linear is suspected</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Less powerful than Breusch-Pagan when the real "
"heteroscedasticity is in fact simple and linear</td></tr><tr><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Goldfeld-Quandt</td><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Difference in residual variance between two ordered zones of the "
"model</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px "
"solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">When a clear break point in variance is "
"suspected along an ordering variable</td><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Requires choosing the ordering variable and omitting a central band "
"of data</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Spearman |residuals| "
"vs fitted</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: "
"1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Whether residual magnitude grows or "
"shrinks monotonically with fitted values</td><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Exploratory, nonparametric, easy-to-read diagnostic</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Only captures monotonic relationships; not a formal "
"test in the sense of the previous three</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"
msgstr ""

#: assumptionLibrary/results/independenceTable.content
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box;\"><table "
"style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 0.5em 0 0.8em 0; "
"font-size: 0.95em;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px "
"8px; border-bottom: 2px solid #999; font-weight: 700;\">Test</th><th "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 2px solid #999; "
"font-weight: 700;\">What it tests</th><th style=\"text-align: left; padding: "
"4px 8px; border-bottom: 2px solid #999; font-weight: 700;\">Typical "
"use</th><th style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 2px "
"solid #999; font-weight: 700;\">Main "
"limitation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Durbin-Watson</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">First-order residual "
"autocorrelation</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Simple static linear "
"regression</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Not appropriate if the "
"model includes the lagged dependent variable as a regressor; does not "
"handle higher-order lags well</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Breusch-Godfrey</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Residual "
"autocorrelation up to a chosen order p</td><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Econometric, dynamic, or multi-lag regressions</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">The order p must be chosen</td></tr><tr><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Ljung-Box</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: "
"4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Joint "
"autocorrelation across several lags</td><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Diagnosing residuals of ARIMA/SARIMA and time-series models</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Indicates joint dependence, but does not by itself "
"identify which lag or its shape</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Runs "
"test</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px "
"solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Randomness of the sequence based on sign "
"changes or position relative to a reference</td><td style=\"text-align: "
"left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Nonparametric, exploratory diagnostic</td><td style=\"text-align: "
"left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Does not directly measure lag-based linear "
"autocorrelation</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"
msgstr ""

#: assumptionLibrary/results/homoscedasticityGroupTable.content
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box;\"><table "
"style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 0.5em 0 0.8em 0; "
"font-size: 0.95em;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px "
"8px; border-bottom: 2px solid #999; font-weight: 700;\">Test</th><th "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 2px solid #999; "
"font-weight: 700;\">What it tests</th><th style=\"text-align: left; padding: "
"4px 8px; border-bottom: 2px solid #999; font-weight: 700;\">Typical "
"use</th><th style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 2px "
"solid #999; font-weight: 700;\">Main "
"limitation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Levene</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Equality of variances "
"using absolute deviations from each group's mean</td><td style=\"text-align: "
"left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">General, widely used first check</td><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Based on the mean; less robust than Brown-Forsythe to skewness or "
"heavy tails</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Brown-Forsythe</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Equality of variances using absolute deviations from "
"each group's median</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Preferred alternative "
"to Levene when there is skewness, heavy tails, or outliers</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Slight power loss versus Levene when data are "
"genuinely normal</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px "
"8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Bartlett</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Equality of variances assuming normality within each "
"group</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px "
"solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">When per-group normality is reasonable "
"and the most powerful test is wanted</td><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Very "
"sensitive to non-normality; may flag heterogeneity that is actually "
"non-normality</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Fligner-Killeen</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Equality of variances "
"via a rank-based test on the deviations</td><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Non-normal data, outliers, or when the most robust alternative is "
"wanted</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: "
"1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Lower power than Levene or Bartlett "
"when the data are in fact approximately normal</td></tr><tr><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Hartley's Fmax</td><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Ratio between the largest and smallest group variance</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Quick descriptive indicator, especially in balanced "
"designs</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: "
"1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Not a formal test with a well-defined "
"distribution outside balanced designs</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"
msgstr ""

#: assumptionLibrary/results/normalityTable.content
msgid ""
"<div style=\"max-width: 7.25in; width: 100%; box-sizing: border-box;\"><table "
"style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 0.5em 0 0.8em 0; "
"font-size: 0.95em;\"><thead><tr><th style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px "
"8px; border-bottom: 2px solid #999; font-weight: 700;\">Test</th><th "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 2px solid #999; "
"font-weight: 700;\">What it tests</th><th style=\"text-align: left; padding: "
"4px 8px; border-bottom: 2px solid #999; font-weight: 700;\">Typical "
"use</th><th style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 2px "
"solid #999; font-weight: 700;\">Main "
"limitation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Shapiro-Wilk</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">General normality, the "
"battery's primary test</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Small or moderate "
"sample; the default reference test</td><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">In R "
"it is only computed for up to 5000 observations (Shapiro &amp; Wilk, "
"1965)</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Lilliefors</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Normality via K-S with corrected critical "
"values</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: "
"1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">When an alternative to Shapiro-Wilk "
"based on the cumulative distribution is needed</td><td style=\"text-align: "
"left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">The classic (uncorrected) K-S test is not valid here; the corrected "
"version is always used (Lilliefors, 1967)</td></tr><tr><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Anderson-Darling</td><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Normality with more weight on the tails</td><td style=\"text-align: "
"left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">When heavy tails or extreme values are a concern</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Can be overly sensitive to trivial tail deviations "
"with very large samples</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: "
"4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Cramér-von "
"Mises</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px "
"solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Normality comparing the entire cumulative "
"distribution</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">When deviations are "
"suspected to be spread across the central body, not at one point</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Less used and less familiar than Shapiro-Wilk or "
"Anderson-Darling in applied practice</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: "
"left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Shapiro-Francia</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">A Shapiro-Wilk variant "
"with a different calculation</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px "
"8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Moderate or large "
"samples, as complementary evidence to Shapiro-Wilk</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Its practical advantage over Shapiro-Wilk is marginal "
"in many scenarios (Shapiro &amp; Francia, 1972)</td></tr><tr><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Pearson chi-square</td><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Normality by comparing observed and expected frequencies by "
"interval</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: "
"1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">As additional secondary evidence, not "
"as the primary test</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Depends on how the "
"data are grouped into intervals; tends to be less powerful than "
"full-distribution tests</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: "
"4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Jarque-Bera</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Normality by jointly "
"evaluating skewness and kurtosis</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: "
"4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Moderate or "
"large samples</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Less stable and "
"informative in very small samples (Jarque &amp; Bera, "
"1987)</td></tr><tr><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Skewness test</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Skewness of the distribution only</td><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Point diagnostic on the direction of skewness (long "
"left or right tail)</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Evaluates only one "
"aspect of shape; does not replace a general normality test</td></tr><tr><td "
"style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; "
"vertical-align: top;\">Kurtosis test</td><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Peakedness and tail weight only</td><td style=\"text-align: left; "
"padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: "
"top;\">Point diagnostic on heavy tails, light tails, or a concentration "
"different from normal</td><td style=\"text-align: left; padding: 4px 8px; "
"border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd; vertical-align: top;\">Evaluates only one "
"aspect of shape; does not replace a general normality "
"test</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"
